Exactly. Coordinates can tell us where characters meet, and their paths can tell us who they are.

Two walks to (2,3) by counting two steps right and three up. Three gets there by listening for the third note from the balcony. Same spot, different things they’ll remember when the lights go out. Two can retrace the route. Three can tell who’s still in the room.

And the map can have a point of view. To King One, that seat is (2,3) from the brass peg. To the horn player at (3,-2), it’s (-1,5) from her chair. So you can write a scene where two characters give different directions to the same place, accuse each other of being wrong, and then realize they’ve been starting from different seats.

FOUR: Everyone is to use the official origin.
HORN PLAYER: Can the official origin see over the trombones?
FOUR: The official origin is a peg.
HORN PLAYER: Then it can sit here.

That’s a whole ensemble show. The map connects the cast, the route gives each character a way of solving things, and changing whose chair we sit in changes what we can see.